# GitHub
Anyone on GitHub, a person or an organization, and any repository, found by what you type.
Every thing it shows is in GitHub.api.spell, as `spell place ingest` wrote it;  each type below adds to its type there,
with `<spell-more-of>`, so looking again at the server never touches a line of this.
A page in a browser holds no key:  it may search 10 times a minute, so the field waits for you to stop typing.

<spell-thing "github page">
	<spell>
		- its "search" is text
		- its "showing" is a user
		- to create it:
			set its search to "solid"
	</spell>

	<action "try (words as text)">
		set its showing to nothing
		set its search to words
	</action>

	<draw>
		<stack spacing="xl">
			<form value={it}>
				<stack spacing="sm">
					<stack direction="horizontal" wrap align="center" justify="between" spacing="sm">
						<header size="large">People and code, on GitHub</header>
						<ui-search name="search" parameter="who" icon="search" placeholder="A person, an organization, a repository" aria-label="Search GitHub" />
					</stack>
					<toolbar appearance="secondary" size="small" aria-label="Try a search">
						<text slot="title" size="small" color="grey">Try</text>
						<item onClick={try "ryansolid"}>ryansolid</item>
						<item onClick={try "solidjs"}>solidjs</item>
						<item onClick={try "vite"}>vite</item>
						<item onClick={try "torvalds"}>torvalds</item>
						<item onClick={try "spreadsheet"}>spreadsheet</item>
					</toolbar>
				</stack>
			</form>
			{draw the found of it}
			{draw the issues of it}
			<text size="small" paragraph>
				From <link link="https://docs.github.com/en/rest">GitHub's REST API</link>, asked for as you look.
			</text>
		</stack>
	</draw>

	<draw "issues" description="Solid's newest issues and pull requests, told apart:  GitHub lists both as issues, and only a pull request has a pull request">
		<stack spacing="sm">
			<header size="medium">Solid's issues</header>
			<for the first 6 issues where its pull request is nothing as="cards" columns="3" doubling stackable loading="Asking GitHub" empty="No open issues." />
			<header size="medium">Solid's pull requests</header>
			<for the first 6 issues where its pull request is not nothing as="cards" columns="3" doubling stackable loading="Asking GitHub" empty="No open pull requests." />
		</stack>
	</draw>

	<draw "found" description="Someone's own page, while one is picked;  else what the search finds">
		<if its showing>
			draw the page of (the user whose id is the login of its showing)
			while waiting:
				<loader visible inline text>Asking GitHub</loader>
		<otherwise>
			<stack spacing="xl">
				<stack spacing="sm">
					<header size="medium">People and organizations</header>
					<for every user matching its search as="cards" columns="4" doubling stackable paging="pages" loading="Asking GitHub" empty="Nobody on GitHub goes by that." />
				</stack>
				<stack spacing="sm">
					<header size="medium">Repositories</header>
					<for every repository matching its search as="cards" columns="3" doubling stackable paging="pages" loading="Asking GitHub" empty="No repository matches that." />
				</stack>
			</stack>
		</>
	</draw>
</spell-thing>


# Someone on GitHub
A person or an organization:  a card in a search, and a page of their own, with their bio and their most popular repositories.
A search gives only a little of each:  their page reads them whole, by their login.

<spell-more-of "user">
	<action "look at it" description="Their own page, in the GitHub tab">
		set the showing of the github of the public servers to it
	</action>

	<action "look away from it" description="Put their page away again, with its Back button">
		set the showing of the github of the public servers to nothing
	</action>

	<draw>
		<ui-card onClick={look at it}>
			<content>
				<stack direction="horizontal" align="center" spacing="sm">
					<image src={its avatar url} alt="" size="mini" appearance="circular" />
					<stack spacing="none" align="start">
						<header>
							{its login} <link link={its html url} target="_blank" aria-label="On GitHub"><icon name="github" /></link>
						</header>
						<meta>{its kind}</meta>
					</stack>
				</stack>
			</content>
		</ui-card>
	</draw>

	<draw "page">
		<stack spacing="xl">
			<stack direction="horizontal" align="center" spacing="sm">
				<button icon="arrow left" appearance="basic" aria-label="Back" title="Back" onClick={look away from it} />
				<header size="large">
					{its name if its name otherwise its login} <link link={its html url} target="_blank" aria-label="On GitHub"><icon name="github" /></link>
				</header>
			</stack>
			<stack direction="horizontal" wrap align="start" spacing="md">
				<image src={its avatar url} alt="" size="small" appearance="rounded" />
				<stack spacing="sm">
					<text color="grey">{its login} · {its kind}</text>
					<if its bio>
						<text>{its bio}</text>
					</if>
					<!-- A search gives no counts:  only the record read by its login has them, and its date with them -->
					<if its created at>
						<text size="small" color="grey">{its public repos} public repositories · {its followers} followers</text>
					</if>
				</stack>
			</stack>
			<stack spacing="sm">
				<header size="medium">Most popular</header>
				<for every repository matching ("user:" + its login + " sort:stars") as="cards" columns="3" doubling stackable paging="pages" loading="Asking GitHub" empty="Nothing public yet." />
			</stack>
		</stack>
	</draw>
</spell-more-of>


# An issue
GitHub lists a pull request among a repository's issues:  this page tells them apart by its "pull request".

<spell-more-of "issue">
	<draw>
		<ui-card>
			<content>
				<header>
					{its title} <link link={its html url} target="_blank" aria-label="On GitHub"><icon name="github" /></link>
				</header>
				<meta>#{its number} · {its state}</meta>
			</content>
		</ui-card>
	</draw>
</spell-more-of>


# A repository
Its name, what it's for, and how many people starred it;  its GitHub icon opens it on GitHub, in a new window.

<spell-more-of "repository">
	<draw>
		<ui-card>
			<content>
				<header>
					{its full name} <link link={its html url} target="_blank" aria-label="On GitHub"><icon name="github" /></link>
				</header>
				<meta>★ {its stargazers count}<if its language> · {its language}</if></meta>
				<description>{its description}</description>
			</content>
		</ui-card>
	</draw>
</spell-more-of>

